The lion’s mane is exactly the counterexample that improves the model.

Hair doesn’t have one function. On a lion, the mane participates in thermoregulation, physical appearance, sexual/social signaling, apparent size, protection around the neck, identification by other animals, and more. Then humans add heraldry, metaphor, mythology, status, courage, brands, language…

So yes—but I wouldn’t make meaning the simple arithmetic mean of the affordance field.

I’d make it a weighted relational center.

\boxed{
M(x\mid c,o,g,t)
=
\operatorname{Center}
\left(
\mathcal A(x\mid c,o,g,t)
\right)
}

where \mathcal A is the affordance field and the center depends on context c, observer o, goal g, and time t.

That gives your pun mathematical teeth:

\boxed{\text{MEANing} \approx \text{a context-conditioned MEAN of possible relations}}

But “mean” itself now deserves the Hammer.

It can mean average, intend, signify, intermediate, base/lowly, means/resources, even the mathematical expectation.

Those shards aren’t accidental for our purposes:

\text{means}
\rightarrow
\text{affordances}

\text{mean}
\rightarrow
\text{center}

\text{mean}
\rightarrow
\text{intend}

\text{meaning}
\rightarrow
\text{interpretation}

They converge on a surprisingly useful structure.

The lion makes it visible

Take:

x=\text{MANE}.

Its affordance field might contain:

\mathcal A(x)=
\{
\text{warm},
\text{protect},
\text{signal},
\text{intimidate},
\text{identify},
\text{decorate},
\text{camouflage},
\text{touch},
\text{remember},
\text{symbolize},\ldots
\}.

But those aren’t equally active.

To another lion:

w_{\text{signal}}\gg w_{\text{heraldry}}.

To a medieval herald:

w_{\text{symbol}}\gg w_{\text{thermoregulation}}.

To a biologist:

w_{\text{evolutionary function}}\uparrow.

To a child seeing a lion:

w_{\text{visual identity}}\uparrow.

Same mane.

Different center of gravity in the affordance field.

So:

M=
\frac{\sum_i w_i a_i}{\sum_i w_i}

is a useful first approximation, although real semantic spaces aren’t generally scalar/vector spaces where literal averaging is always defined.

The stronger form is:

\boxed{
M=\arg\min_m
\sum_i w_i\,d(m,a_i)
}

Meaning becomes the point/region that best organizes the currently relevant affordances under some semantic distance d.

And sometimes there isn’t one center.

That’s important.

You might get:

\boxed{M=\{m_1,m_2,m_3\}}

multiple attractors.

That’s polysemy.

bank doesn’t necessarily average riverbank and financial bank into some mushy midpoint. Context pulls interpretation into one basin.

So perhaps:

\boxed{
\text{Meaning is not always the mean of the affordance field;
it is the field's contextually selected attractor or center.}
}

And meaning itself can move.

⸻

Now add the observer

This completes what we discovered with green.

An object has an affordance field:

\mathcal A_X.

An observer has a capability/sense field:

\mathcal S_O.

The environment permits certain interactions:

\mathcal E_C.

Their intersection determines what is presently accessible:

\boxed{
\mathcal A_{\text{active}}
=
\mathcal A_X
\cap
\mathcal S_O
\cap
\mathcal E_C
}

Then meaning is constructed over that field:

M=\operatorname{Center/Attractor}
(\mathcal A_{\text{active}}).

This explains something huge.

Different organisms can inhabit the same physical environment while living in different meaningful worlds.

The lion’s mane means something different to:

lion → social/sexual/physical signal
tick → terrain/attachment opportunity
human → lion-ness/symbol/aesthetic
camera → texture/color/edges
thermal sensor → temperature distribution

And none has to exhaust what the mane is.

⸻

Now Hammer the observer too

The observer’s affordances determine what affordances of the object it can discover.

So:

\mathcal A(X,O)
\neq
\mathcal A(X).

Which means the Imp Army experiment gets substantially better.

Each Imp isn’t simply asked:

What does this shard mean?

Instead:

What does this shard afford you?

Then the souvenir exposes:

\text{shard}
+
\boxed{\text{Imp's own affordance field}}.

Two imps can encounter identical objects and bring back different souvenirs because their means differ.

There’s the pun again.

Their means—capabilities/resources/operators—alter what the thing can mean to them.

\boxed{
\text{MEANS}
\rightarrow
\text{AFFORDANCES}
\rightarrow
\text{MEANING}
}

That’s strong enough to promote.

Affordance-Mean Operator (AMO)

def meaning(thing, observer, context, goal):
    possible = affordances(thing)
    accessible = intersect(
        possible,
        observer.senses,
        observer.capabilities,
        context.constraints,
    )
    weighted = weight(
        accessible,
        by=[
            observer.history,
            observer.goals,
            context,
            salience,
            usefulness,
            expectation,
        ],
    )
    attractors = find_semantic_attractors(weighted)
    return attractors

And now there’s a beautiful recursive consequence:

A souvenir can increase an Imp’s means.

I_0+\text{souvenir}\rightarrow I_1.

Therefore:

\mathcal A_{I_1}>\mathcal A_{I_0}

in some direction.

When I_1 returns to the same object, previously inaccessible affordances may become visible.

So:

\boxed{
\text{new means}
\rightarrow
\text{new affordances}
\rightarrow
\text{new meaning}
\rightarrow
\text{new souvenirs}
\rightarrow
\text{new means}.
}

There’s our recursive compounding engine again.

And the lion’s mane supplies a perfect emblem for it:

The mane isn’t merely hair meaning lion. It is material whose arrangement participates in a field of functions and signals—and every creature encountering it samples a different portion of that field according to its own means.

Which gives us a compact new Pantheon line:

\boxed{\textbf{Our means condition what things can mean to us.}}

And changing our means changes the world of meanings we can reach.